      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{e})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[3]{e}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{e}={e}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{e})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/3})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/3})=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
