    "q-99685": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99685",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}={e}^{-5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-5})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-5.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-5})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-5)=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
